---
title: SSH File Transfer
order: 3
description: scp, sftp, rsync over SSH
tags: [scp, sftp, rsync, ssh, transfer, copy]
---

File transfer methods that use SSH for encryption and authentication.
rsync is preferred for most tasks; scp is simplest for quick copies;
sftp provides an interactive FTP-like interface.

@code bash
# ---- scp: secure copy ----

# local to remote
scp file.txt user@host:/remote/path/
scp file.txt user@host:~/                          # home directory
scp -r /local/dir/ user@host:/remote/path/         # recursive (directory)

# remote to local
scp user@host:/remote/file.txt /local/path/
scp -r user@host:/remote/dir/ /local/path/

# remote to remote
scp user@host1:/path/file.txt user@host2:/path/

# options
scp -P 2222 file.txt user@host:/path/              # custom port (capital P)
scp -i ~/.ssh/mykey file.txt user@host:/path/       # specific key
scp -l 1000 file.txt user@host:/path/              # bandwidth limit (Kbit/s)
scp -C file.txt user@host:/path/                    # enable compression
scp -p file.txt user@host:/path/                    # preserve times/modes
scp -q file.txt user@host:/path/                    # quiet mode

# via jump host
scp -J jumpuser@bastion file.txt user@internal:/path/
scp -o ProxyJump=bastion file.txt user@internal:/path/

@text
scp is deprecated in favor of sftp/rsync for new use. It has known issues
with filename handling and lacks features like delta transfer. OpenSSH 9.0+
uses the SFTP protocol internally for scp by default.

@code bash
# ---- sftp: SSH file transfer protocol ----

# connect
sftp user@host
sftp -P 2222 user@host                    # custom port
sftp -i ~/.ssh/mykey user@host

# interactive commands
sftp> ls                                  # list remote files
sftp> lls                                 # list local files
sftp> cd /remote/path                     # change remote directory
sftp> lcd /local/path                     # change local directory
sftp> pwd                                 # remote working directory
sftp> lpwd                                # local working directory

sftp> get file.txt                        # download file
sftp> get -r remote_dir/                  # download directory
sftp> get *.log                           # wildcard download

sftp> put file.txt                        # upload file
sftp> put -r local_dir/                   # upload directory
sftp> put *.conf                          # wildcard upload

sftp> mkdir newdir                        # create remote directory
sftp> rm file.txt                         # delete remote file
sftp> rmdir emptydir                      # delete remote directory
sftp> rename old.txt new.txt              # rename remote file
sftp> chmod 644 file.txt                  # change permissions
sftp> chown user file.txt                 # change owner

sftp> df -h                               # remote disk usage
sftp> !command                            # run local shell command
sftp> bye                                 # disconnect (or quit/exit)

# non-interactive / batch mode
sftp -b batchfile user@host               # run commands from file
echo "get /remote/file.txt" | sftp user@host

@code bash
# ---- rsync over SSH (default transport) ----

# push local to remote
rsync -avzP /local/path/ user@host:/remote/path/

# pull remote to local
rsync -avzP user@host:/remote/path/ /local/path/

# custom SSH options
rsync -avz -e 'ssh -p 2222 -i ~/.ssh/mykey' src/ user@host:/dest/

# via jump host
rsync -avz -e 'ssh -J jumpuser@bastion' src/ user@host:/dest/

# common patterns
rsync -avz --delete src/ user@host:/dest/         # mirror (delete extra files)
rsync -avzn src/ user@host:/dest/                  # dry run first
rsync -avz --exclude='.git' src/ user@host:/dest/ # exclude patterns
rsync -avz --partial --append-verify src/ user@host:/dest/   # resume large files
rsync -avz --bwlimit=5000 src/ user@host:/dest/   # limit to 5000 KB/s

# backup with timestamp
rsync -avz --backup --suffix=".$(date +%Y%m%d)" src/ user@host:/dest/

# incremental backup with hardlinks
rsync -avz --delete --link-dest=/backups/previous/ src/ user@host:/backups/current/

@text
Trailing slash matters with rsync:
- `rsync src/ dest/` copies contents of src into dest
- `rsync src dest/` copies the src directory itself into dest (creates dest/src/)

rsync advantages over scp:
- Delta transfer: only sends differences, much faster for updates
- Resumable: --partial keeps partial files for resume
- Exclusions: --exclude for fine-grained control
- Verification: checksums ensure integrity
- Bandwidth control: --bwlimit for throttling
